{
  "title": "Mastodon \u2014 social network / federated microblogging (49,962\u2605)",
  "content": "Project: Mastodon\nDomain: social network / federated microblogging | Scale: large | Stars: 49,962\nProblem: Mastodon provides a free, open-source, self-hosted social network server based on ActivityPub that enables users to run their own federated microblogging communities without vendor lock-in, interoperable across the broader Fediverse.\nTarget users: Individual users seeking privacy-respecting social media, Server administrators running federated instances, Third-party app developers via REST API, Fediverse participants on non-Mastodon software\nCore features: ActivityPub federation - seamless interoperability across federated servers; Real-time chronological timeline with WebSocket streaming; Media attachments - image and video upload and playback; Safety and moderation tools - private posts, blocking, muting, reporting; OAuth2 and REST API for third-party application ecosystem; Multi-language support and internationalization via Crowdin; Docker and docker-compose deployment configurations\nIntegrations: ActivityPub protocol, WebFinger, OAuth2 providers, BrowserStack for cross-browser testing, Chromatic for visual regression testing\nTech stack: React.js with Redux, TypeScript, SCSS, Vite + Ruby on Rails + PostgreSQL 14+ + Node.js 22+ for streaming API, Redis 7.0+ for caching/queueing, Sidekiq for job processing, Docker, FFmpeg 5.1+ for media processing\nKey libraries: Rails, React, Redux, Vite, Vitest, TypeScript, Sidekiq, @dnd-kit, ESLint, Stylelint\nArchitecture: microservices\nDesign lessons:\n- Decoupled frontend (React/Vite) from backend (Rails) with separate workspace tooling enables independent scaling of API and UI concerns\n- Real-time capabilities delegated to Node.js streaming server rather than Rails, following polyglot architecture for performance\n- Federation via ActivityPub standard allows interoperability without centralization, demonstrating protocol-driven architecture over proprietary APIs\n- Comprehensive testing strategy combining unit tests, visual regression (Chromatic), cross-browser testing, and linting ensures quality for a critical social infrastructure project",
